okay we're going to begin with breaking  news out of chat where the armies  announced president idris debbie has  been  killed the army says he was leading  soldiers fighting rebels on the front  line  debbie had just won re-election and was  one of africa's longest  serving leaders hiber morgan's joining  us now live from chad's capital in jama  heba what more do we know about what's  been happening  well rob what we do have right now is a  statement that came from the army itself  which is that  uh president debbie who's been running  the country for more than 30 years  and who was set to lead it for another  five years  passed away from injuries he sustained  over the weekend in the fight between  the government forces  and the rebel group known as the front  for change and concord in the northern  region of canaan  now let's go back to how this all  unfolded on april 11th on the day of the  election  uh where debbie was running for a sixth  term uh the rebels came in from libya  where they have a non-aggression pact  with the  uh libyan warlord khalifa after and  launched an attack on a government  station at the border region of tibesti  and then they went on to advance until  they reached the region of canaan which  is about 300 kilometers  from the capital in germany now the  statement from the army at the time was  that they had launched airstrikes  against the rebel groups to prevent them  from advancing  uh to the region of canada which is  where they were able to reach up until  on saturday of just this weekend uh  the government then went on to assure  the citizens who were panicking here in  the capital in germany that everything  was okay  and that there was no need to concern  and that the rebels will not reach the  capital  like they had announced and we also know  that uh president debbie himself went to  the front lines  along with top army officials to uh lead  the battles against uh the group  uh and announced later that at least 300  were killed and more than 140  were captured while the rest fled and  were being pursued by the government  military  now we hadn't heard from debbie uh over  the weekend we didn't hear from him  yesterday as well on the day  that the uh national electoral  commission announced that he had won the  uh election by nearly 80 percent uh we  we were expecting to hear a statement  from him uh to uh to to  assure his supporters and to uh  basically welcome the results of the  national electoral commission  that now will not be happening we know  that uh we just learned from the army  that he had sustained injuries during  that  battles over the weekend and those were  the reasons and the bad the  injuries were the reasons why he passed  away so he will not be leaving the  country for the next  five years obviously the news is just  coming out and we're trying to assess  what's going on but is there any sense  from where you are  of how the country is going to be  reacting to this  well the capital has been very tense rob  even before the announcement that their  president  has passed away over the past couple of  days with the announcement that the  rebels were advancing towards in germany  lots of stores around the capital were  closed people were mostly staying  indoors  because they were concerned about the  security situation that only increased  yesterday morning when they saw more  government troops  patrolling the streets saying that they  were worried that these are signs that  the government statements  that the rebels have been repelled and  that they were not advancing may not be  correct and that they could be some  threats to their safety so lots of  people opted to stay  indoors and lots of stores opted to  remain closed now last night we heard  celebratory gunfires  around the capital in germany despite  the army saying that that should not be  the case because of  the panic mode that the people were in  but we heard a celebratory gun fine  nonetheless  around the capital in germany today  things were quite normal in the morning  we've seen stores opening  people were out and about they seemed to  be a little bit more assured  after seeing that debbie was poised to  leave for another five years and that  the army has been repelled  as per the government statement we yet  to see how they will react to this they  were very assured  this morning they were in a panic mode  yesterday evening and yesterday morning  so things have been very fluid here in  the capital in germany  but thanks very much indeed that's  hibern talking to us from chad's capital  in jama i want to bring in  um ami didris whose life for us in abuja  has been covering this region for us for  for many years  um the the western countries have often  seen  idris debbie as really a key ally in the  fight against  um armed groups like boko haram for  example and of course  now the front for changing concord in  chat what do you think  is going to be the impact of this news  on the security situation in the region  well basically rob let's start with the  chad itself now succession battle starts  constitutionally the speaker of  parliament is supposed to take over  if the president is indisposed now  whether or not  that will happen uh it remains to be  seen because this is a country  that is um that has seen a lot of coups  a lot of rebellions uh internally within  its own military  and of course the rebellion in the north  as well now regionally  charges involved in peacekeeping  operations or operations against armed  groups  in the lake chart region in central  africa and other parts of  the region as well so chad for many  countries  in the region is an important ally in  the fight against uh  extremist group or armed groups that  have been operating in that region  for example china played a critical role  in displacing boko haram from its  territory last year  after an operation led by the president  himself just like the way he did in the  north  he led a force that dislodged boko haram  fighters and iso  fighters from the lectured region  freeing the territory of chad of these  attackers  and he's been a crucial partner for  nigeria a lot of chinese forces are  currently  are part of the multinational joint task  force working in nigeria working in asia  working in parts of cameroon  as well as other parts of the region to  keep ice swap and boko haram away  this will mean a very very tense  situation for the region  uh especially countries that are  struggling to deal with these armed  groups  so it's it's very very critical now  whoever succeeds  um child or mr president idris debbie  uh should quickly address the issue or  the vacuum that may likely arise in case  in  in in the in in the incidence of his  death now so  nigeria for example i know is very  worried uh it needs to  handle the situation very very well  currently we've seen an uptick in  violence in  the lectured region bordering child as  well as parts of  the northeast of nigeria apart from  nigeria chat or  republic also is dealing with an optic  in violence uh  crossing from mali into niger from  burkina faso  and cameroon also has its own share of  violence in  the recent past so this is a very very  tense moment for  all countries in the region that have in  one way or the other relied on child  for collaboration in dealing with armed  groups in the region  we were talking there ahmed about the  the uh succession process but we're just  seeing on chad tv  that the army is being quoted as saying  that a military council  is going to be handling that transition  now  given the fact that people in chad had  really hoped  that this was going to be the end of a  fairly prolonged political process and a  fairly strenuous one  how do you think that that news is going  to go down  for the politicians it's a very very  heavy blow to democracy whether or not  president idris debbie was a dictator  whether or not he manipulated  the electoral system that could 